Absolutely! In fact, that's one of the biggest advantages of tunneling.

A tunnel is **not** a deployment. Think of it as a **window into your laptop**.

```
Friend's Browser
        │
        ▼
Public URL
        │
        ▼
Tunnel
        │
        ▼
Your Node.js Server (localhost:3000)
        │
        ▼
Your Source Code
```

So when you modify your code, the tunnel simply forwards requests to the **latest version** running on your laptop.

---

## Backend changes

Suppose you add:

```javascript
app.post("/pin_message", ...)
```

Save the file.

Restart Node.js (or let `nodemon` restart it automatically).

Everyone using the tunnel immediately starts using the new backend.

---

## Frontend changes

Suppose you edit:

```text
chat_style.css
```

or

```text
chat_script.js
```

Save.

Refresh the browser.

Everyone sees the updated frontend.

No need to recreate the tunnel.

---

## I actually recommend using `nodemon`

Instead of

```bash
node index.js
```

run

```bash
nodemon index.js
```

Now every time you save your backend:

```
Save index.js

↓

Node automatically restarts

↓

Tunnel continues working
```

No manual restart.

---

## Example workflow

Tomorrow you'll have something like:

```
VS Code
│
├── Edit JS
├── Edit HTML
├── Edit CSS
└── Save
      │
      ▼
Node Restarts (nodemon)
      │
      ▼
Tunnel
      │
      ▼
Everyone sees the changes
```

It's actually a very pleasant development experience.

---

## One small thing

If you change the frontend, users who already have the page open will need to **refresh** to download the new HTML/CSS/JS.

If you change the backend, the next request they make will automatically use the new backend.

